Raise your hands, let me see.

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.270
Raise 'em high, don't be scared,

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:08.580
some of you are scared,
get it higher, baby.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.730
Let me tell you how to do that.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:11.860
I'm gonna help you right now.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:13.820
I would like you to eat healthier,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.940
less calories and cleaner,

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.250
and I would like you to go

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.820
to the gym and do physical exercise.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:21.460
But what do people want?

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.440
They want to take some fucking
apple vinegar gummy pills.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.770
If they've got paper, they want surgery.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.277
Same with business.

00:00:31.277 --> 00:00:33.130
This shit is simple.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:35.380
Do the right things, the
right things will happen.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.860
But people are always looking
for fucking shortcuts.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.490
People are looking to
point fucking fingers

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.860
instead of thumbs, that's
how our society's anxious.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.260
Gratitude will make you happy every day

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:46.130
and make your business
better, you know why?

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:48.630
'Cause as we sit here, right this second,

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:51.590
there are 850 million people on earth

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:54.570
that do not have access to clean water.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:58.090
This is a mindset game,
this is a perspective game.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:59.320
You wanna find positivity?

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:00.650
I do it for a living.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:01.483
Guess what?

00:01:01.483 --> 00:01:03.100
It's there 24/7.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.040
You wanna be happy?

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:04.880
It's unlimited.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:07.717
You choose what life is.

00:01:07.717 --> 00:01:09.566
That's how I think about business.

00:01:09.566 --> 00:01:14.400
Anything you do that is not
nice is costing you money

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.829
and the faster you
figure that actually out

00:01:16.829 --> 00:01:20.220
and the faster you figure
out why you're not nice,

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.560
the more likely you'll be successful.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.670
I'm really happy to be here.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.240
I'm really looking forward
to the fireside chat

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.695
because I really want it to
be contextual to the industry.

00:01:29.695 --> 00:01:32.314
Obviously, given that my sister's in it,

00:01:32.314 --> 00:01:34.620
I've always paid a lot of attention to it

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.680
because I always feel that
there's so much ambition

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:41.010
and entrepreneurial tendencies
in the real estate industry.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:43.830
But I also have always
been fascinated by it

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.958
because it's similar to the
reason I like the NFT market

00:01:46.958 --> 00:01:49.370
and why I always loved collectibles,

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.550
'cause I'm fascinated by
the concept of markets

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:52.723
going up and down.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.450
Especially, if you break down my content,

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.100
I think so much of it
speaks to being practical

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:02.630
and being self aware and
staying in your lane.

00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:06.240
And people really get
caught in this industry

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.310
by being overexcited when times are good

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.780
and not anticipating when times are bad.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.170
And so, it's been really
interesting to watch

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:15.423
over the last 30 years, the way to win,

00:02:15.423 --> 00:02:19.800
how easy it is to lose, and
there's so many variables in it.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.400
The thing that I probably
want to talk most about is

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.300
what I've been spending a
lot of my time on lately

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:28.150
which is, what does it take,

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.090
from an emotional intelligence standpoint,

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.340
to actually be successful?

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.360
I think the concept of soft skills

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.360
has been really fascinating to me.

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:42.348
That when we talk about empathy
or kindness or patience,

00:02:42.348 --> 00:02:45.630
we consider them soft skills.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.800
And I truly believe that

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:51.030
they're the actual hard
skills of business,

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.910
that knowing how to write up a contract,

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:58.910
or use Excel, or like run the
math is actually quite easy.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.810
What I think is very hard is

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:04.900
when another human being
is incredibly disrespectful

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.160
to your face and having
the intestinal fortitude

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.370
to be the bigger person in that moment

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:13.010
for what you're trying to accomplish.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.100
And I think it's time

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.740
that we really start
having this conversation

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:19.363
in the business world.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.080
How many people here have employees?

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.740
Raise your hand, raise it high.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:25.573
Thank you.

00:03:25.573 --> 00:03:26.406
So for example,

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:27.940
for all the people that
just raised their hand,

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:30.150
something I've been thinking
a lot about lately is

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.324
the concept of, does anybody here

00:03:33.324 --> 00:03:36.276
actually have the explanation

00:03:36.276 --> 00:03:41.276
to why it is ever appropriate
for a boss to scream

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:43.840
and yell at somebody in a negative way

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:45.807
in front of other people?

00:03:45.807 --> 00:03:46.640
- [Man] Never.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.010
- Like, yeah, we're all
like, "No, no, no, never."

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:55.374
But it happens every fucking day.

00:03:55.374 --> 00:03:59.490
And I've been fascinated by
it because I'm like, wow,

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:04.490
for a long time, the context
of doing business has been used

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:09.660
as an excuse framework for
just bad behavior, and comma,

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:13.030
and here's the most interesting part,

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:15.700
I'm stunned by how many
people are confused

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:19.683
that fear is a better motivator than love.

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:23.770
I'm fascinated by that.

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:27.810
You know, the hypothesis of
my new book and how I wrote it

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.050
and really what I think about

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:31.299
and really COVID gave me the opportunity

00:04:31.299 --> 00:04:34.550
to really finally put on paper

00:04:34.550 --> 00:04:36.470
what's been on the tip of my tongue,

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.280
what's always been in my stomach,

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:42.888
what's been back here,
but I got it out there is

00:04:42.888 --> 00:04:45.950
I'm trying to build the
biggest empire in the world.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:46.783
I wanna be the single,

00:04:46.783 --> 00:04:49.840
most successful entrepreneur
of all time financially.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.940
Like the points, the money,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.033
I have no concern about saying that

00:04:54.033 --> 00:04:58.920
but I'm stunned by the
fact that people feel that

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.063
you have to compromise your
values to be successful.

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:07.185
I remember when Steve
Jobs got wildly popular

00:05:07.185 --> 00:05:10.450
in Silicon Valley, and I
would be at conferences

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:11.770
and I would talk about

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:15.620
how I was so marveled by his
incredible innovation skills

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:18.480
but I didn't love the way the
narrative was being painted

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:20.070
about how he treated employees.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:23.086
And it was like, I was
watching kids, young kids,

00:05:23.086 --> 00:05:25.570
who were starting apps and companies,

00:05:25.570 --> 00:05:28.300
genuinely almost think it was cool

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.440
to be tough on their people,
not that they were so bad,

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:33.430
but they felt like this
was gonna motivate them.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:35.635
And I was very affected by it.

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:39.300
And I think that it's
really led to a place where,

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:43.749
for me, as my profile and
the attention on me has grown

00:05:43.749 --> 00:05:46.580
that I feel incredibly compelled

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:49.190
to have the conversation
of my management style.

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:50.830
It's why I had to go vulnerable,

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:53.970
which is not my favorite
move, with this book

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.250
'cause in "Twelve and a Half",

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:58.810
I talk about my half being candor,

00:05:58.810 --> 00:06:01.990
Gary Vee on stage and
in content is a beast

00:06:01.990 --> 00:06:04.590
when it comes to candor, it's my strength.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:08.640
Gary Vaynerchuk as an
operator was really bad at it.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:11.070
I actually just genuinely didn't like

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.750
the feeling of delivering bad news,

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:14.626
telling someone they weren't
good at what they did.

00:06:14.626 --> 00:06:16.900
I didn't value money enough

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.250
so I'd be willing to keep people around.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.420
I tried a hundred different ways to fix it

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.590
without telling them that they stink.

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:25.160
And then, what would ultimately happen is

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.550
I would get resentment.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.940
I would hold on for dear
life for three years.

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:30.590
And then, finally on a random Tuesday,

00:06:30.590 --> 00:06:32.856
surprise the shit out
of them and fire them.

00:06:32.856 --> 00:06:34.397
(audience laughing)

00:06:34.397 --> 00:06:39.110
Which is the complete scenario of why

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:41.700
anybody who's ever worked
for me does not like me

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:43.570
in a world where that
shouldn't be the case

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:46.500
based on who I am and how I value money.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.650
And I had to go through
that in my late 30s

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:52.420
and early 40s, even probably
early forties and recently.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:56.150
And what has transpired once
I kind of tricked myself,

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:58.305
I almost Gary Vee'd myself

00:06:58.305 --> 00:07:01.780
and basically put the word
kind in front of candor

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:04.310
and I've managed that way
for the last two years,

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:05.660
and for anybody, and I saw a lot of you

00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:09.274
with your body language
who have that shortcoming,

00:07:09.274 --> 00:07:12.870
it has been staggering how effective

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:16.690
kind candor has been for me,
and has been foundational

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:18.790
in the growth of VaynerX and VaynerMedia

00:07:18.790 --> 00:07:20.750
over the last 24 months.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:23.618
Other people, candor comes very natural.

00:07:23.618 --> 00:07:26.903
The problem is they deliver
it like a dick face,

00:07:27.999 --> 00:07:30.310
and that's never gonna work.

00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:33.280
You know, genuinely making
someone not feel good,

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.980
genuinely scaring someone
is a losing formula.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.130
You can more than easily
use it in the short term

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:42.760
to capture some cash.

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.300
I promise you,

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:47.323
you will leave money on the
table deploying that format.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:52.300
And it's an important
convo because we have not,

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:55.160
in popular culture, had the conversation

00:07:55.160 --> 00:08:00.050
that warm and kind and nice
is the actual alpha skill

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:03.540
to make lots of money, and
I'm fucking here for it

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.276
and I hope you're ready for it.

00:08:05.276 --> 00:08:08.526
(audience applauding)

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:12.857
It's a big deal 'cause my book isn't like,

00:08:12.857 --> 00:08:14.810
"Let's all go to a mountain and drink tea

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:16.737
and pray nice shit will happen."

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:19.432
This is not a fucking delusional,

00:08:19.432 --> 00:08:21.420
like it's all gonna be okay.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:24.883
This is if you wanna dominate long term.

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:27.450
The reason so many people lose

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:30.200
in the real estate market
overall, they will in NFTs,

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:31.220
they do in collectibles

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:34.310
and many other things
is they want money fast

00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:35.860
and they do short term behavior

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:40.470
and they don't thoughtfully
think long term, right?

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:42.613
They overextend themselves, game over.

00:08:43.764 --> 00:08:46.643
That's how I think about
emotional intelligence.

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:49.800
If you play in a way

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:52.420
where every relationship
appreciates you and,

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:54.950
listen, do you understand
what I'm talking about

00:08:54.950 --> 00:08:55.900
when I talk about strength?

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.360
I'm actually not, I love this subject

00:08:58.360 --> 00:08:59.699
'cause I wanna get deeper into it.

00:08:59.699 --> 00:09:02.210
Of course, it's easy to be nice to someone

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:04.060
who's nice to you.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:05.853
I'm talking about the other shit.

00:09:06.905 --> 00:09:08.220
Everyone's like, "Yeah, I'm nice."

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:09.880
I'm like, "But what about last Thursday

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:12.077
when you all wanted to
punch Patty in the face?"

00:09:12.077 --> 00:09:14.358
"Well, she was a bitch,
I'm like, 'fuck you'."

00:09:14.358 --> 00:09:15.200
(audience laughing)

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:16.780
I'm like, "That's what I'm talking about."

00:09:16.780 --> 00:09:19.643
Not when shit's easy, when shit's hard.

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:25.170
Everybody in this room has
likely had someone get cute

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.223
and not pay you for some shit.

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:29.100
What do you do then?

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:34.270
How do you have the strength emotionally

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:35.693
to be the bigger person?

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:41.040
Do you waste your energy
on envy and jealousy

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:43.890
or talking shit about that person?

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:47.100
Or are you on the offense
and just moving on?

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:49.240
This is money talk.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:52.830
This isn't grandma's nice
talk, this is money talk.

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:54.120
And that's the nuance

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:57.320
that I'm trying to bring to
forefront of the conversation.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:01.450
I am going to make
uncomfortable levels of money

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:04.103
on the foundation of kindness.

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:07.810
When I dissect what's going on with me

00:10:07.810 --> 00:10:12.080
and how it's happening, it
is grounded in patience.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:14.660
It is grounded in empathy.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:18.630
It is grounded in compassion.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:20.963
It is grounded in that stuff.

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:25.040
People are like, "Gary, this is all wrong.

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:26.960
I'm nice and people walk all over me."

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:29.214
I'm like, "Let's break it down, Becky.

00:10:29.214 --> 00:10:31.130
(audience laughing)

00:10:31.130 --> 00:10:32.593
Like, tell me how you're nice."

00:10:32.593 --> 00:10:34.500
Now, let me tell you
about the conversations

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:35.630
when I broke down the people

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:36.990
that tell me they get walked all over

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:38.650
and that's why you can't be nice.

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:40.250
Let me make it real simple for everybody.

00:10:40.250 --> 00:10:41.770
Here's what's actually happening.

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:43.220
It's one of two things.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.913
One, they're actually
trying to manipulate people.

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:50.467
This whole conversation of,
"I get walked all over."

00:10:50.467 --> 00:10:53.560
No, no, you tried to
give somebody some shit

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:55.120
that they didn't even want

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:57.857
because you wanted something from them.

00:10:57.857 --> 00:10:59.860
"Gary Vee, see you're coming to Dallas,

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:01.850
let me give you a ride to the hotel

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:05.032
to save you some money on the car."

00:11:05.032 --> 00:11:08.530
Translation, "Hey dude,
I wanna pitch you shit

00:11:08.530 --> 00:11:10.847
for 35 minutes while I
get you to the hotel."

00:11:13.270 --> 00:11:14.473
That happens every day.

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:17.660
People out here trying to act kind,

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:20.410
they're using it as manipulation.

00:11:20.410 --> 00:11:23.170
Like, don't give me shit I don't want.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:25.370
And then, ask immediately after.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:28.345
That's not kind, that's manipulation.

00:11:28.345 --> 00:11:29.590
That's number one.

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:34.590
Or number two, you're kind,
but you're scared to negotiate.

00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:38.450
You're insecure and it
has nothing to do with

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:41.840
you being kind, you're
getting walked all over

00:11:43.194 --> 00:11:45.130
because you're unhappy

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:48.113
that somebody's negotiating in business.

00:11:50.395 --> 00:11:52.895
You just genuinely wish
you could get more for it.

00:11:53.930 --> 00:11:55.253
You could have said no.

00:11:57.370 --> 00:11:58.620
We lack accountability.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.637
I had this talk the other day, like,

00:12:00.637 --> 00:12:02.020
"But I had to sell it for 40."

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:04.670
I was like, "But you said yes,

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:06.970
you could have told
Harold to go fuck himself.

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:11.100
You said yes, nobody walked all over you.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:13.487
You folded 'cause it
was a good deal for you

00:12:13.487 --> 00:12:15.157
or you needed to move it."

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:18.573
I'm fascinated by what's
going on in the world.

00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:22.040
We've gotten into such a funny place

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:23.823
for a million different reasons.

00:12:25.100 --> 00:12:27.660
And I just want people
to lock in on themselves

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:30.280
and realize how much opportunity you have.

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:31.330
- [Man] Yeah.

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:32.830
- The biggest thing I'd like to leave with

